      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0061",
      "sequence": 916,
      "meaning": "At the camp Rāma looks upon Lakṣmaṇa and speaks in human fashion. Midnight has passed and Hanumān has not come. Lifting his brother to his breast, Rāma recalls Lakṣmaṇa’s tender nature: unable to bear seeing him grieve, he left parents and endured forest, cold, heat and wind for Rāma’s sake. Why does that love no longer answer? Had Rāma known separation from a brother awaited in exile, he would not have obeyed his father. Sons, wealth, wives and homes recur, but a brother born from the same womb cannot be replaced. Without Lakṣmaṇa, life is like a bird without wings, serpent without jewel or elephant without trunk. How can he return to Ayodhyā having lost his brother for a woman? He could bear slander and a wife’s loss, but not Sumitrā’s bereavement: she entrusted her only son to him. What answer can he give? Tears stream from his lotus-petal eyes as Śiva explains that indivisible Raghurāya displays human emotion from compassion for devotees. The monkeys despair at his lament—then Hanumān arrives like heroic ardour within compassion.",
